-- title:
--   Theorem 3.2, proof, claim (3.2.1) — the sextic $p$ is coercive
-- statement:
--   Let $p(x)=17x_1^6-20x_1^4+7x_1^2+18x_1+18x_2^4-19x_2^2-19x_2+21-20x_1x_2$ be the polynomial of Theorem 3.2. Then $p$ is **coercive**:
--   $$
--   p(x)\to+\infty\qquad\text{as }\|x\|\to\infty,\ x\in\mathbb R^2 .
--   $$
--   Together with claim (3.2.2) this gives the nonnegativity of $p$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: coercive: f(xₖ) → +∞ along every sequence with ‖xₖ‖ → +∞ → Tendsto (fun x => eval x p) (cocompact (Fin n → ℝ)) atTop; reason: in finite dimension the cocompact filter is generated by complements of bounded sets, so the two definitions coincide
-- source:
--   Ahmadi, Dibek & Hall, Sums of Separable and Quadratic Polynomials, arXiv:2105.04766v1, Theorem 3.2, proof, claim (3.2.1), p.10

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SPQPoly_p32

open MvPolynomial Filter

namespace SPQPoly

/-- **Claim (3.2.1): the polynomial of Theorem 3.2 is coercive.**
Ahmadi, Dibek & Hall, *Sums of Separable and Quadratic Polynomials*, arXiv:2105.04766v1, Theorem 3.2, proof, claim (3.2.1), p.10: "(3.2.1) p(x) is coercive."

**Formalization Note.** `p` is `p32`. Coercive is
`Tendsto (fun x => eval x p32) (cocompact (Fin 2 → ℝ)) atTop`: `p32(x) → +∞` as `‖x‖ → ∞`. -/
theorem claim_3_2_1 :
    Tendsto (fun x : Fin 2 → ℝ => eval x p32) (cocompact (Fin 2 → ℝ)) atTop := by sorry

end SPQPoly
